(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endo1-practice-quiz"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology I — Practice Exam (119 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement BEST describes an endocrine hormone?","choices":["Diffuses only to neighboring cells","Never enters the circulation","Acts only on the cell that secretes it","Travels through the bloodstream to distant target organs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Endocrine hormones enter the bloodstream and act on distant target organs; that circulatory delivery is the defining feature. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which type of signaling is demonstrated when nitric oxide diffuses to nearby vascular smooth muscle?","choices":["Paracrine","Autocrine","Neuroendocrine","Endocrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. NO diffusing to adjacent smooth muscle is paracrine — a local mediator acting on neighboring cells. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A cytokine released by an immune cell acts back on that SAME immune cell. This is an example of:","choices":["Paracrine","Neuroendocrine","Autocrine","Endocrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A signal acting back on the same cell that released it is autocrine. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Oxytocin released from the posterior pituitary is classified as:","choices":["Intracrine","Autocrine","Paracrine","Neuroendocrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A neuron (hypothalamic) secreting a hormone into the blood is neuroendocrine signaling. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone travels directly through plasma WITHOUT requiring a carrier protein?","choices":["Insulin","Cortisol","Aldosterone","T4"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Insulin is a water-soluble peptide, so it dissolves freely in plasma without a carrier protein. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone requires a plasma carrier protein?","choices":["Epinephrine","Growth Hormone","Glucagon","Testosterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Testosterone is a lipophilic steroid, poorly soluble in plasma, so it requires a carrier protein. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Water-soluble hormones generally bind receptors located:","choices":["On mitochondrial membranes","On the plasma membrane","Within the Golgi apparatus","Inside the nucleus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Water-soluble hormones can't cross the membrane, so their receptors sit on the cell surface. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Lipid-soluble hormones generally produce their effects by:","choices":["Opening sodium channels","Activating membrane ion channels only","Releasing calcium from lysosomes","Activating nuclear transcription"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lipid-soluble hormones cross the membrane and bind nuclear receptors that alter gene transcription. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is MOST likely to have the fastest onset of action?","choices":["Thyroxine (T4)","Epinephrine","Cortisol","Testosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine acts through membrane receptors and second messengers, giving near-instant onset. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone class is synthesized from cholesterol?","choices":["Glycoproteins","Catecholamines","Peptides","Steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Steroid hormones are all synthesized from cholesterol. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Growth hormone belongs to which hormone class?","choices":["Steroid","Thyroid hormone","Catecholamine","Peptide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. GH is a peptide hormone. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is synthesized from the amino acid tyrosine but behaves like a steroid hormone?","choices":["Dopamine","T3","Norepinephrine","Epinephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. T3 is made from tyrosine but is lipophilic and acts on nuclear receptors like a steroid. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is synthesized from tyrosine but behaves as a water-soluble hormone?","choices":["T4","Epinephrine","Testosterone","Cortisol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine is made from tyrosine but is water-soluble and acts at membrane receptors. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Steroid hormones are generally:","choices":["Stored in secretory vesicles","Highly water soluble","Synthesized on demand","Released by exocytosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Steroids aren't stored — they're synthesized on demand from cholesterol as needed. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Peptide hormones are generally:","choices":["Made only when needed","Stored in vesicles","Bound to intracellular receptors","Lipid soluble"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Peptide hormones are pre-made and stored in secretory vesicles for rapid release. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which pairing is CORRECT?","choices":["Testosterone — nuclear receptor","Cortisol — membrane receptor","Epinephrine — nuclear receptor","Insulin — nuclear receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Testosterone is a steroid that binds intracellular/nuclear receptors. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is MOST likely to alter gene transcription directly?","choices":["Insulin","T3","Glucagon","ADH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. T3 is lipophilic and binds nuclear receptors to directly change gene transcription. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The MAIN advantage of carrier proteins for steroid hormones is:","choices":["Increased plasma solubility and longer half-life","Increased filtration by kidneys","Faster receptor binding","Faster degradation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carriers keep lipophilic steroids soluble in plasma and extend half-life by protecting them from clearance. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Compared with peptide hormones, steroid hormones generally have:","choices":["Shorter half-lives","Longer half-lives","Faster onset of action","No plasma transport"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protein-bound steroids resist rapid degradation, giving longer half-lives than peptides. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A hormone that crosses the plasma membrane WITHOUT a membrane receptor is MOST likely:","choices":["Insulin","Cortisol","Epinephrine","Glucagon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol is a lipophilic steroid that diffuses across the membrane to intracellular receptors. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Peptide hormones are synthesized primarily in the:","choices":["Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi only","Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Mitochondria only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peptides are proteins synthesized on ribosomes of the rough ER. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Steroid hormones are synthesized primarily from:","choices":["Cholesterol","Tyrosine","Triglycerides","Glycogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Steroid hormones are synthesized from cholesterol. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"After secretion, peptide hormones are generally degraded by:","choices":["Plasma enzymes","Intracellular lysosomes only","Bone marrow","Adipose tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peptide hormones are broken down quickly by plasma and tissue enzymes (proteases). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The liver plays an important role in:","choices":["Producing ADH","Metabolizing many hormones","Synthesizing insulin","Producing TSH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The liver metabolizes and inactivates many hormones. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The kidneys contribute to hormone clearance primarily by:","choices":["Producing cortisol","Hormone synthesis","Filtration and excretion","Producing growth hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The kidneys clear hormones by filtration and urinary excretion. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The MOST common regulatory mechanism in endocrinology is:","choices":["Feed-forward regulation","Positive feedback","Negative feedback","Neural inhibition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Negative feedback is the dominant regulatory mechanism in the endocrine system. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which is the BEST example of positive feedback?","choices":["Thyroid hormones suppressing TSH","Cortisol inhibiting ACTH","Estrogen causing the LH surge","Insulin lowering blood glucose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The estrogen-driven LH surge is the classic positive-feedback example. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient receives a large dose of exogenous cortisol for several weeks. Which hormone is MOST likely to decrease?","choices":["Epinephrine","ACTH","Glucagon","Insulin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exogenous cortisol suppresses the HPA axis, lowering ACTH via negative feedback. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which sequence BEST illustrates a classic endocrine negative feedback loop?","choices":["Hormone rises > Target response > Hormone rises further","Hormone rises > Target organ destroyed","Hormone rises > Target response > Hormone secretion decreases","Hormone falls > Target response > Hormone falls further"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Classic negative feedback: a rising hormone triggers a response that then reduces further secretion. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"⭐ Integration: A researcher develops a synthetic hormone that easily crosses cell membranes, travels bound to plasma proteins, alters gene transcription, and has a prolonged half-life. This hormone MOST closely resembles:","choices":["Insulin","Epinephrine","Glucagon","Cortisol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A membrane-crossing, protein-bound, transcription-altering, long-lived hormone is a steroid — cortisol fits best. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is directly controlled by the:","choices":["Pineal gland","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus","Adrenal cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pituitary is controlled by the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The anterior pituitary develops embryologically from:","choices":["Mesoderm","Rathke's pouch","Neural ectoderm","Neural crest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The anterior pituitary arises from Rathke's pouch (oral ectoderm). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The posterior pituitary develops from:","choices":["Mesoderm","Endoderm","Rathke's pouch","Neural tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The posterior pituitary is neural tissue — a downgrowth of the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Communication between the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary occurs primarily through:","choices":["Sympathetic nerves","Lymphatics","Cranial nerves","Portal blood vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypothalamic hormones reach the anterior pituitary via the hypophyseal portal veins. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal system allows:","choices":["Growth hormone to reach bone","ADH to enter urine","Small amounts of hypothalamic hormones to directly reach the anterior pituitary","Hormones to bypass the bloodstream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The portal system delivers tiny amounts of hypothalamic releasing hormones directly to the anterior pituitary. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell secretes Growth Hormone?","choices":["Lactotroph","Gonadotroph","Somatotroph","Corticotroph"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. GH is made by somatotrophs. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell secretes ACTH?","choices":["Corticotroph","Lactotroph","Somatotroph","Thyrotroph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ACTH is made by corticotrophs. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cells release FSH and LH?","choices":["Somatotrophs","Corticotrophs","Lactotrophs","Gonadotrophs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FSH and LH are made by gonadotrophs. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cells release prolactin?","choices":["Corticotrophs","Lactotrophs","Gonadotrophs","Thyrotrophs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prolactin is made by lactotrophs. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"TSH is secreted by:","choices":["Somatotrophs","Thyrotrophs","Lactotrophs","Corticotrophs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. TSH is made by thyrotrophs. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Growth Hormone-Releasing Hormone (GHRH) primarily stimulates:","choices":["ACTH","Prolactin","GH","TSH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. GHRH stimulates GH release. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Somatostatin primarily inhibits secretion of:","choices":["GH","ADH","ACTH","Oxytocin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Somatostatin inhibits GH (and TSH). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"TRH stimulates release of:","choices":["TSH (and prolactin)","GH","ACTH only","FSH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. TRH stimulates TSH — and also prolactin. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"CRH stimulates release of:","choices":["GH","ACTH","TSH","ADH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. CRH stimulates ACTH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"GnRH stimulates secretion of:","choices":["TSH","LH and FSH","GH","Prolactin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. GnRH stimulates LH and FSH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Dopamine primarily inhibits secretion of:","choices":["TSH","GH","Prolactin","ACTH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Growth Hormone secretion is greatest during:","choices":["REM sleep","Deep slow-wave sleep","After large glucose meals","Afternoon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. GH secretion is greatest during deep slow-wave sleep. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which of the following stimulates GH secretion?","choices":["Hyperglycemia","High circulating IGF-1","Exercise","Obesity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exercise stimulates GH secretion. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Growth Hormone is secreted by the:","choices":["Thyroid gland","Somatotrophs","Adrenal cortex","Posterior pituitary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. GH is secreted by somatotrophs of the anterior pituitary. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Most growth-promoting effects of GH occur through:","choices":["Thyroxine","Cortisol","Glucagon","IGF-1"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most GH growth effects are mediated by IGF-1 from the liver. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"IGF-1 is produced primarily by the:","choices":["Pancreas","Thyroid","Kidney","Liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. IGF-1 is produced primarily by the liver. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Growth Hormone directly increases:","choices":["Glucose uptake into muscle","Insulin sensitivity","Protein synthesis","Fat storage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. GH directly increases protein synthesis (anabolic on protein). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Growth Hormone promotes:","choices":["Lipogenesis","Protein degradation","Glycogen breakdown only","Lipolysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. GH promotes lipolysis (fat breakdown for fuel). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Growth Hormone tends to increase blood glucose because it:","choices":["Decreases glucose uptake by peripheral tissues","Increases insulin sensitivity","Stimulates insulin secretion only","Decreases hepatic glucose production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. GH is diabetogenic — it decreases peripheral glucose uptake, raising blood glucose. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Because of its effects on glucose metabolism, GH is sometimes described as:","choices":["Mineralocorticoid","Thyromimetic","Anti-inflammatory","Diabetogenic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because it raises blood glucose, GH is called diabetogenic. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone provides the major negative feedback to suppress GH secretion?","choices":["Cortisol","T3","ACTH","IGF-1"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. IGF-1 provides negative feedback to suppress GH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient exercises vigorously for one hour. What is the expected effect on GH secretion?","choices":["Increase","Complete suppression","No change","Decrease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vigorous exercise increases GH secretion. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient receives recombinant IGF-1 therapy. Which hypothalamic response is MOST likely?","choices":["Increased GnRH","Increased Dopamine","Increased GHRH","Increased Somatostatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exogenous IGF-1 increases hypothalamic somatostatin, suppressing GH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A child has extremely low GH levels before epiphyseal plate closure. Which finding is MOST likely?","choices":["Short stature with delayed growth","Acromegaly","Gigantism","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low GH before growth-plate closure causes short stature with delayed growth. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"⭐ Integration: A patient receives chronic injections of recombinant Growth Hormone. Which sequence BEST describes the endocrine response?","choices":["↑ IGF-1 → ↑ Somatostatin → ↓ endogenous GH secretion","↓ IGF-1 → ↑ GHRH → ↑ GH","↓ Somatostatin → ↑ ACTH","↑ TRH → ↑ TSH → ↑ GH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic exogenous GH raises IGF-1, which increases somatostatin and suppresses endogenous GH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Unlike the anterior pituitary, the posterior pituitary primarily:","choices":["Synthesizes peptide hormones","Secretes TSH and ACTH","Stores and releases hormones produced by the hypothalamus","Produces steroid hormones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The posterior pituitary stores and releases hormones (ADH, oxytocin) made in the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"ADH is synthesized primarily in the:","choices":["Anterior pituitary","Supraoptic nucleus of the hypothalamus","Pineal gland","Posterior pituitary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ADH is synthesized in the supraoptic nucleus of the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Oxytocin is synthesized primarily in the:","choices":["Paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus","Posterior pituitary","Adrenal medulla","Thyroid gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxytocin is synthesized in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Hormones traveling from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary are transported along:","choices":["Portal veins","Capillaries only","Lymphatics","Axons"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ADH and oxytocin travel down axons (axonal transport) to the posterior pituitary. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Neurophysins primarily function to:","choices":["Stimulate ADH secretion","Activate aquaporins","Transport ADH and oxytocin down axons","Increase GFR"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neurophysins are carrier proteins that escort ADH/oxytocin down the axons. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The posterior pituitary releases:","choices":["GH","ADH and oxytocin","ACTH","TSH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the anterior from the posterior pituitary?","choices":["The anterior pituitary stores hypothalamic hormones.","The posterior pituitary stores hypothalamic hormones and releases them into circulation.","The posterior pituitary secretes ACTH.","Both synthesize all of their own hormones."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The posterior pituitary stores hypothalamic hormones and releases them into circulation. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Damage to the hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract would MOST directly decrease secretion of:","choices":["GH","TSH","ADH","Prolactin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The tract carries ADH to the posterior pituitary, so damage most directly reduces ADH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The primary stimulus for ADH secretion is:","choices":["Hypercalcemia","High plasma osmolarity","Hyperglycemia","Low plasma osmolarity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The primary trigger for ADH release is high plasma osmolarity. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A significant decrease in blood volume also stimulates:","choices":["Increased ADH release","Increased insulin","Increased glucagon","Decreased ADH release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A large drop in blood volume also stimulates ADH release. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"ADH primarily acts on which nephron segment?","choices":["Bowman's capsule","Loop of Henle","Proximal tubule","Collecting duct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ADH acts on the collecting duct. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"ADH binds primarily to which receptor in the kidney?","choices":["8, receptor","V2 receptor","a, receptor","Muscarinic receptor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ADH binds V2 receptors in the kidney. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Activation of V2 receptors causes insertion of:","choices":["ENaC channels","Aquaporin-2 channels","NKCC2 transporters","GLUT4 transporters"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. V2 activation inserts aquaporin-2 channels, increasing water reabsorption. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The major physiological effect of ADH is:","choices":["Increased water reabsorption","Increased glucose filtration","Increased sodium excretion","Increased potassium secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ADH's main effect is increased water reabsorption. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient with central diabetes insipidus will MOST likely produce urine that is:","choices":["Large volume and dilute","Rich in protein","Rich in glucose","Small volume and concentrated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central DI (no ADH) produces large volumes of dilute urine. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which laboratory finding is MOST consistent with untreated diabetes insipidus?","choices":["Hyponatremia","Hypernatremia with concentrated urine","Hypernatremia with dilute urine","Low plasma osmolarity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Untreated DI causes water loss → hypernatremia with inappropriately dilute urine. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient receives desmopressin (synthetic ADH). Which change is expected?","choices":["Increased urine volume","Decreased water reabsorption","Decreased urine volume","Increased plasma osmolarity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Desmopressin (ADH analog) increases water reabsorption, decreasing urine volume. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which condition is characterized by excessive ADH secretion?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism","Diabetes insipidus","SIADH","Addison disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. SIADH is excessive ADH secretion. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient with SIADH will MOST likely have urine that is:","choices":["Concentrated","Dilute","Protein-rich","Glucose-rich"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In SIADH, excess ADH concentrates the urine. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Compared with normal, plasma sodium in SIADH is typically:","choices":["Extremely elevated","Unchanged","Decreased","Increased"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. SIADH causes water retention and dilutional hyponatremia (low sodium). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Oxytocin primarily stimulates:","choices":["Glucose uptake","Water reabsorption","Milk ejection","Sodium retention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxytocin drives milk ejection (let-down). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which cells contract during the milk let-down reflex?","choices":["Myoepithelial cells","Podocytes","Osteoblasts","Alveolar epithelial cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myoepithelial cells contract to eject milk. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"During labor, cervical stretch causes:","choices":["Positive feedback","Decreased uterine contractions","Inhibition of oxytocin","Negative feedback"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cervical stretch triggers positive feedback during labor. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for the uterine contraction positive feedback loop during childbirth?","choices":["ADH","Prolactin","Cortisol","Oxytocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oxytocin drives the uterine-contraction positive-feedback loop of childbirth. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The milk let-down reflex is triggered primarily by:","choices":["Infant suckling","Increased plasma glucose","Increased cortisol","Elevated TSH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infant suckling triggers the let-down reflex. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for milk production?","choices":["Oxytocin","Prolactin","ADH","GH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prolactin produces milk. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for milk ejection?","choices":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","FSH","Estrogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oxytocin ejects milk. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A woman cannot eject milk despite normal milk production. Which hormone is MOST likely deficient?","choices":["Oxytocin","Growth Hormone","ACTH","Prolactin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Milk made but not ejected implies oxytocin deficiency (ejection failure). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which of the following represents a classic example of positive feedback?","choices":["Oxytocin increasing uterine contractions during labor","Cortisol suppressing ACTH","Thyroid hormone suppressing TSH","Insulin lowering blood glucose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxytocin increasing uterine contractions in labor is positive feedback. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"⭐ Integration: A patient suffers a traumatic injury that destroys the posterior pituitary but leaves the hypothalamus intact. Which combination of findings is MOST likely?","choices":["↓ ADH release, ↓ oxytocin release, polyuria","↑ ADH, concentrated urine","↑ Prolactin, galactorrhea","↓ GH, ↓ ACTH, hypothyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Losing the posterior pituitary removes ADH and oxytocin release → polyuria (DI) plus impaired let-down. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The functional unit of the thyroid gland is the:","choices":["Follicle","Islet","Acinus","Lobule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The functional unit of the thyroid is the follicle. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Thyroid follicles are primarily lined by:","choices":["Cuboidal epithelial cells","Squamous epithelium","Columnar smooth muscle","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Follicles are lined by cuboidal epithelial cells. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The lumen of the thyroid follicle contains:","choices":["Plasma","Calcitonin only","Colloid rich in thyroglobulin","Blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The follicle lumen holds colloid rich in thyroglobulin. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The primary function of thyroglobulin is to:","choices":["Serve as the scaffold for thyroid hormone synthesis","Transport iodine into cells","Transport T3 in plasma","Activate TSH receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thyroglobulin is the scaffold on which thyroid hormone is built. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which transporter actively moves iodide from the blood into thyroid follicular cells?","choices":["GLUT4","NKCC2","Sodium-Iodide Symporter (NIS)","ENaC"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Iodide is pulled into the follicular cell by the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"After entering the follicular cell, iodide is transported into the colloid primarily by:","choices":["ROMK","Aquaporin-2","Pendrin","NCC"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Iodide crosses into the colloid via pendrin. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which enzyme oxidizes iodide and catalyzes iodination of tyrosine residues?","choices":["ACE","Carbonic anhydrase","Renin","Thyroid peroxidase (TPO)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) oxidizes iodide and iodinates tyrosines. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The process of adding iodine to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin is called:","choices":["Organification","Proteolysis","Phosphorylation","Hydrolysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adding iodine to tyrosine residues is organification. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which combination forms T3?","choices":["DIT + DIT","T4+TSH","MIT + MIT","MIT + DIT"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. T3 = MIT + DIT. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which combination forms T4?","choices":["DIT + DIT","MIT + MIT","MIT + DIT","T3 + TSH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. T4 = DIT + DIT. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Most circulating thyroid hormone is bound primarily to:","choices":["Thyroxine-Binding Globulin (TBG)","Hemoglobin","Cortisol-binding globulin","Albumin only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone is carried mostly by thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Only what fraction of thyroid hormone is biologically active?","choices":["Protein-bound","Colloid-bound","Intracellular only","Free (unbound)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only free (unbound) hormone is biologically active. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Compared with T3, T4:","choices":["Is produced outside the thyroid gland","Is more biologically active","Has a longer half-life","Has a shorter half-life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. T4 has a longer half-life than T3 (T3 is more potent but shorter-acting). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Most circulating T3 is produced by:","choices":["Adrenal cortex","Posterior pituitary","Peripheral conversion of T4","Direct thyroid secretion only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most T3 comes from peripheral conversion of T4. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The enzyme responsible for converting T4 to T3 is:","choices":["Thyroid peroxidase","Deiodinase","Renin","Carbonic anhydrase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Deiodinase converts T4 to T3. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Thyroid hormone enters target cells primarily by:","choices":["Gap junctions","Diffusion/transport into cells followed by nuclear receptor binding","Receptor-mediated endocytosis","Exocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone enters cells and binds nuclear receptors. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The thyroid hormone receptor is located primarily:","choices":["In the nucleus","On the plasma membrane","In mitochondria only","In lysosomes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The thyroid hormone receptor is in the nucleus. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Thyroid hormone exerts its effects primarily by:","choices":["Increasing gene transcription","Releasing calcium from the SR","Activating G-proteins","Opening sodium channels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone acts by increasing gene transcription. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which effect is expected with increased thyroid hormone?","choices":["Increased oxygen consumption","Decreased heat production","Decreased basal metabolic rate","Decreased ATP utilization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. More thyroid hormone raises oxygen consumption and metabolic rate. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Thyroid hormone generally causes:","choices":["Tachycardia","Complete heart block","AV block","Bradycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone increases heart rate (tachycardia). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which cardiovascular effect is associated with excess thyroid hormone?","choices":["Reduced myocardial contractility","Decreased cardiac output","Increased cardiac output","Decreased B-receptor sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Excess thyroid hormone raises cardiac output. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Thyroid hormone generally increases:","choices":["Gastrointestinal motility","Body weight","Protein storage only","Plasma cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone increases GI motility (hence diarrhea in hyperthyroidism). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient with untreated hyperthyroidism is MOST likely to have:","choices":["Heat intolerance","Bradycardia","Cold intolerance","Weight gain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hyperthyroidism causes heat intolerance (plus weight loss and tachycardia). — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Thyroid hormone is essential for normal:","choices":["Red blood cell production only","Antibody synthesis","Platelet formation","Brain development in infancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thyroid hormone is essential for infant brain development. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A deficiency of thyroid hormone during infancy can result in:","choices":["Cushing syndrome","Diabetes insipidus","Congenital hypothyroidism with impaired neurologic development","Acromegaly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Infant thyroid deficiency causes congenital hypothyroidism with neurologic impairment. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic hormone stimulates TSH secretion?","choices":["GHRH","TRH","GnRH","CRH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. TRH stimulates TSH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary hormone directly stimulates the thyroid gland?","choices":["ACTH","GH","Prolactin","TSH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. TSH directly stimulates the thyroid gland. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"High circulating T3 and T4 suppress secretion of:","choices":["TSH only","Both TRH and TSH","TRH only","ACTH only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High T3/T4 feed back to suppress both TRH and TSH. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient has primary hypothyroidism caused by thyroid gland failure. Which hormone profile is MOST likely?","choices":["↑ T3/T4, ↑ TSH","↑ T3/T4, ↓ TSH","↓ T3/T4, ↓ TSH","↓ T3/T4, ↑ TSH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary hypothyroidism: gland fails → low T3/T4, and TSH rises from loss of feedback. — Endocrine Physiology I lecture"}]};
